Titre$ KEY TO THE EUROPEAN SPECIES OF SECT. MULTIFORMES   -   TOR ERIK BRANDRUD & AL.
Com$ Journal des J.E.C. No. 16., p. 162 – 199, 2014   -   Adapté par Paul Bertéa 07-12-2015

Note: The differentiation in macrocharacters is little or little known between a number of closely related species. Therefore, microcharacters (spore characters) are emphasized in the key. The often occurring honeysmell is most pronounced in bulb context. 
1,2 $ In deciduous forests. Pileus when young pale ochraceous yellow (-honey brown) to almost whitish (if more ochre brown, then spores small, MV < 8.5 µm)
1,5 $ In coniferous (mainly Picea) forests. Pileus when young darker ochraceous brown-fulvous to vividly apricot-red brown
2,0 $ Pileus initially silvery-frosty at centre, distinctly innately fibrillose towards margin; almost whitish, later (pale) ochraceous yellow; sometimes more ochre brown. Robust species with a distinct marginate bulb. Smell strong of honey. Spores variable, (7?)7.5–9.5 × 4.5–5.5 µm; northern, subalpine Betula pubescens populations have small spores (normally <8.5 µm long). Widespread, mainly under Betula, Fagus, Quercus, Carpinus, Corylus $ C. talus
2,3 $ Pileus not so frosty-innately fibrillose (if innately fibrillose, then northern, with slender stipe, and with larger spores)
3,0 $ Spores relatively large, 9–10(?10.5) × 5–6 µm, amygdaloid; in northern Fennoscandian (boreal/subalpine) mixed Betula pubescens forests. Pileus initially (very) pale and somewhat innately fibrillose. Basidiomes slender, bulb indistinct; smell of honey $ C. pallidirimosus
3,4 $ Spores small, 7–8.5 × 4–5 µm; in thermophilous Quercus-Corylus-Fagus forests; pileus weakly innately fibrillose or not; basidiomes robust, with a distinctly marginate bulb
4,0 $ Spores narrowly amygdaloid, (7.5?)8–8.5(?9) × (4?)4.5–5 µm; pileus pale honey brown to more grey brown or flesh-coloured; no smell of honey noted. In Quercus-Corylus forests on calcareous, sandy soil. Very rare (S Norway, Estonia) $ C. melleicarneus
4,0 $ Spores ellipsoid-subamygdaloid, 7?8.5(?9) × 4?5(?5.5) µm, with often somewhat coarser, confluent warts; pileus ochraceous yellow to pale ochraceous brown, smell of honey. In rich Quercus-Carpinus-Fagus(-Abies) forests. Very rare (N Italy) $ C. frondosomultiformis
5,6 $ Lamellae and stipe (apex) with bluish tinges when young; strong smell of honey
5,7 $ Without any bluish tinges; honey smell almost absent to (rarely) strong
6,0 $ Spores usually ellipsoid and rather strongly and coarsely verrucose, 8–9(–9.5) × 5–6 µm. Pileus vividly, warm ochraceous brown to red brown, often bicoloured with a dark, hygrophanous outer zone. Mainly in S Scandinavia and C Europe $ C. caesiolamellatus
6,0 $ Spores distinctly (acutely) amygdaloid, weakly and densely verrucose, 8.5–9.5(?10) × (4.5–) 5–6 µm. Pileus less vividly coloured (more fulvous-grey brown), rarely bicoloured. Northern Europe (Fennoscandia) $ C. caesiophylloides
7,0 $ Spores strictly amygdaloid, densely verrucose, (8.5?)9?10 × (4.5?)5–6 mm. Usually robust, with distinctly marginate bulb. Pileus often silvery-fibrillose from thin veil remnants. Honey smell weak or absent. Associated with Picea and Abies, mainly in S Fennoscandia and C Europe $ C. talimultiformis
7,8 $ Spores (broadly) ellipsoid-subamygdaloid, with somewhat coarser (confluent-rounded), diffuse ornaments. Robust or not
8,0 $ Pileus strongly apricot-redbrown, with distinct pattern of dark, hygrophanous spots. Spores ellipsoid-subamygdaloid, 8–9.5 × (4.5–)5–5.5 mm, weakly verrucose to almost smooth. Subcutis (hypoderm) with some distinctly zebra-striped encrusted hyphae. Honey smell weak or absent $ C. rufoallutus
8,9 $ Pileus ochraceous brown-fulvous brown, usually without a pattern of hygrophanous spots. Subcutis (almost) without zebra-striped encrusted hyphae
9,0 $ Spores 8–9.5 × (4.5–)5–5.5(–6) mm, ellipsoid-subamygdaloid; basidiomes often slender and with an indistinct bulb, almost without veil remnants; honey smell weak or absent. Mainly in Northern Europe. Widespread. Associated with Picea $ C. multiformis
9,0 $ Spores 9–10 × 5.5–6.5 µm, (broadly) ellipsoid; basidiomes robust, with distinct, white veil remnants at bulb (and pileus) margin; with a distinct honey smell. Recorded from Picea and Larix-Betula forests. Very rare (E Siberia, E France) $ C. armenicorius
